@flexiarg or3/coordinator-in-your-school
@title An Open Research Coordinator in Your Own School
@audience heads of school, research leads, PVC-RI, OR programme manager
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🧭/引 🏫/校]
@see or3/ask-first-then-bring-the-expert, or2/two-joes-one-mandate

! conclusion: An Open Research Coordinator in Your Own School

  + context: Open research is institutionally sponsored but has no presence inside your School.

  + IF:
    Central open research provision exists — policies, a repository, training, a programme manager — and your School's researchers nonetheless treat open research as somebody else's initiative.

  + HOWEVER:
    Central provision cannot know which of your colleagues is stuck on what. Generic training answers questions nobody in the room asked, and a policy tells people what is required without telling them why it is worth doing. The gap is not information; it is that nobody nearby is available to ask.

  + THEN:
    Give one respected, research-active colleague a named coordinator role in the School, with a modest allocation and no regulatory function. Their first task is diagnosis rather than delivery: assess current practice and awareness, and find where colleagues actually want to know more. Their standing job is to bring the right specialist to those needs, usually inside the existing research seminar rather than as a separate compliance strand. The role works precisely because it is not enforcement — it is "not about instituting new rules, but letting researchers know."

  + BECAUSE:
    A named local person lowers the cost of asking a question you think is too basic, and disciplinary credibility does work that central authority cannot: a colleague who publishes in your field can say which practices matter for your kind of work. Fields with a lived reform history (psychology after the replication crisis) already have the awareness — what they lack is someone local to make it concrete.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Identify a candidate who is research-active in the School's dominant methods and trusted enough to be asked naive questions.]
    next[Protect a small, explicit allocation; an unfunded expectation on top of a full workload decays to nothing.]
    next[For an account of the first year in such a role, contact the Open Research Coordinator in the School of Psychology, Social Work and Public Health.]
